AB  - The magnitude and timing of flood peaks are strongly influenced by the non-linear relationship between flow velocity and discharge. Traditional routing models struggle to account for the variation in flow velocity, particularly with time, due to computational constraints. This study proposes a novel Iterative Routing Model (IRM) that updates flow velocity as a function of streamflow magnitude. The IRM was applied to seven gauging stations in the Godavari River Basin, India. It outperformed two other models used in this study in simulating peak discharge and timing, with the lowest average absolute deviations of 29.98 % and 0.2 days, respectively. The IRM achieved the highest median NSE (0.78) and KGE (0.79) values across all stations. Moreover, the calibrated Manning's roughness from the proposed model appears more realistic compared to that given by other models. Overall, our findings highlight the potential of the proposed model to improve flood peak predictions in large river basins.
